      <h2>How to Add a Logo to a QR Code Without Breaking It</h2>

      <p>When you place a logo in the centre of a QR code, you are covering part of the data modules. Reliable generators compensate by using a higher error-correction level, which builds redundancy into the image so the code still scans even if part of it is hidden.</p>

      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 1:</strong> Keep your logo small. A good rule of thumb is no more than 15-20% of the QR code's total area. Larger logos increase scan failure rates.
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 2:</strong> Use a simple logo with a transparent background. Complex photographs or detailed illustrations don't reduce well at small sizes.
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 3:</strong> Always test-scan before printing. Use your phone's native camera app to confirm the code reads reliably from the screen and from a printed test copy.
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <strong>Tip 4:</strong> Maintain contrast. The foreground modules should be significantly darker than the background. Avoid light-grey codes or coloured backgrounds that clash with your logo.
      </div>

      <h2>When Should You Pay for a QR Code Generator?</h2>

      <p>Free generators are enough for most personal and small-business use. Consider upgrading only if you need:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dynamic QR codes</strong> in large volumes (change the URL without reprinting)</li>
        <li><strong>Scan analytics</strong> with detailed location, device, and time data</li>
        <li><strong>Team collaboration</strong> with multiple users and brand asset libraries</li>
        <li><strong>Bulk generation</strong> of hundreds or thousands of codes at once</li>
        <li><strong>White-label</strong> short domains for marketing campaigns</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For a one-off menu, business card, WiFi sign, or event poster, a free static QR code with your logo is all you need.</p>

